1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,522
LawDiff separates interpretation from execution.

2
00:00:02,522 --> 00:00:08,575
Codex read the supplied source texts and produced structured candidate rules, with

3
00:00:08,575 --> 00:00:10,593
the extraction method recorded.

4
00:00:10,593 --> 00:00:16,646
The public demo runs those candidates through one shared rule engine; browser

5
00:00:16,646 --> 00:00:20,681
answers and submission exports use the same logic.

6
00:00:20,681 --> 00:00:26,735
Schema checks, exact source-span checks, calendar validation, and targeted tests catch structural

7
00:00:26,735 --> 00:00:27,239
errors.

8
00:00:27,239 --> 00:00:30,770
Three-valued logic preserves missing facts without guessing.

9
00:00:30,770 --> 00:00:35,814
City rules require geographic evidence, not a matching postal label.

10
00:00:35,814 --> 00:00:41,363
Evidence added in the browser stays separate from the original sample.

11
00:00:41,363 --> 00:00:47,416
A separate API compiler is implemented, but this presentation does not claim

12
00:00:47,416 --> 00:00:49,434
an unperformed API run.

13
00:00:49,434 --> 00:00:55,487
The remaining hard problem is legal interpretation: traceability supports review; it does

14
00:00:55,487 --> 00:00:56,1000
not certify correctness.
